        },
        "title": "How Energy Systems Influence Climate Change",
        "cta": "",
        "intro": "M6: The Climate Change-Energy Nexus"
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Lesson 1 Overview</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "The current energy system is a major driver of global climate change, <a href=\"https://www.wri.org/insights/4-charts-explain-greenhouse-gas-emissions-countries-and-sectors\"><strong><u>accounting for around 75% of total greenhouse gas emissions</u></strong></a><strong>. </strong>This means transforming how we produce and consume energy is essential, with the world’s ability to meet its climate goals hinging on the energy sector’s ability to<strong> </strong>decarbonize and <a href=\"https://www.un.org/en/climatechange/net-zero-coalition\"><u>to keep global warming to no more than 1.5°C – as called for in the Paris Agreement – emissions need to be reduced by 45% by 2030 and reach net zero by 2050</u></a>. As<strong> </strong><a href=\"https://news.un.org/en/story/2023/07/1139162\"><strong><u>temperatures break records year after year</u></strong></a><strong>, </strong>the case for action has never been stronger."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "lesson_part_cover",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/243_343.webp",
          "caption": ""
        },
        "intro": "M6: The Climate Change-Energy Nexus",
        "title": "Part 1: Emissions Breakdown"
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Part 1 Overview</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Reliable, accurate, and timely data are essential for crafting evidence-based energy policies that drive sustainable development. Without quality data, policy decisions risk being misinformed, leading to ineffective solutions or unintended consequences.<br /><br />According to the <a href=\"https://unstats.un.org/unsd/energystats/methodology/documents/IRES-web.pdf\"><u>International Recommendations for Energy Statistics (UN, 2018)</u></a>, data must meet five critical dimensions of quality to effectively support sound decision-making, as introduced on the next page.."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
