    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Since 1990, the three emissions sources within the energy sector that have grown the fastest are: <a href=\"https://www.climatewatchdata.org/ghg-emissions?apcid=000000000000000000000000&breakBy=sector&chartType=line&end_year=2021&start_year=1990\"><u>electricity and heating, which grew by 88%; transportation, which grew by 66%; and manufacturing and construction, which grew by 60%</u></a>. By contrast, non-energy sectors collectively contribute only one-quarter of global emissions, highlighting the critical need to focus on decarbonizing the energy sector."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/284_631.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>GHG Emissions from Energy Production</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Combustion plays a major role in energy production, especially for power generation and heating. Over 40% of energy-related CO2 emissions come from burning fossil fuels for electricity. In 2022 alone, CO2 emissions from energy combustion grew by 1.3% (423 Mt). However, energy-related greenhouse gas emissions arise from a range of sources beyond combustion, including fugitive methane emissions from oil and gas operations and process emissions from industrial activities. CO2 emissions from energy combustion and industrial processes accounted for 89% of energy-related greenhouse gas emissions in 2022. Total energy-related greenhouse gas emissions increased by 1.0% to an all-time high of 41.3 Gt CO2-eq.<br /><br /> Key factors driving CO2 emissions from electricity generation include how much electricity is produced, the efficiency of generation, the reliance on fossil fuels, and the carbon intensity of those fuels. Specifically, coal-fired power plants were the biggest contributors, responsible for 73% of electricity-sector CO2 emissions. <br /><br />While carbon dioxide (CO2) is the most abundant greenhouse gas, the energy sector also emits non-CO2 greenhouse gases like methane (CH4), nitrous oxide (N2O) and and sulfur hexafluoride (SF₆). These gases, although emitted in smaller quantities, have much higher global warming potentials and different atmospheric lifespans, making them critical to address in climate strategies. <br /><br />"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Methane, the second-largest driver of global warming after CO2, is a significant concern in the energy sector. Oil and gas operations are the biggest sources of methane emissions, while incomplete combustion of <ai prompt=\"Share details about bioenergy.\">bioenergy</ai>—like wood, charcoal, and crop residues—adds to methane emissions. Methane is released both intentionally, through practices like venting and flaring during production and maintenance, and accidentally, through leaks in pipelines, compressors, and valves (fugitive emissions)."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Nitrous oxide (N2O), although released in much smaller quantities, is approximately 300 times more potent than CO2 in terms of global warming potential. The agriculture sector is the largest source of N2O emissions globally, due to the widespread use of fertilizers. In the energy sector, N2O emissions result from fossil fuel combustion, <ai prompt=\"Clarify biomass.\">biomass</ai> burning, and some industrial processes. Despite its relatively lower share, N2O emissions from energy remain an important environmental concern, as they contribute not only to global warming but also to ozone layer depletion."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
