    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/284_640.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>GHG Emissions from Energy Production</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Energy distribution contributes to GHG emissions through the transportation of energy from production sites to end-users. This includes emissions from transporting fuels, building and maintaining infrastructure, and operating energy distribution networks. Apart from these, the inefficiencies inherent in the distribution process add to the GHG emissions. In fact, energy losses during transportation are significant, with approximately 19 exajoules (EJ) lost annually in the process. They represent a dual challenge: wasted energy that could otherwise power economies and the associated emissions resulting from generating that wasted energy. These losses equate to roughly $200 billion each year.<br /><br />A key source of emissions in the transmission and distribution of electricity is sulfur hexafluoride (SF₆). It is used as an insulating gas in high-voltage electrical equipment such as switchgear and circuit breakers and is the most potent greenhouse gas regulated under the Kyoto Protocol. Its GWP is about 25,200 times greater than CO₂, and even small leaks during equipment operation, maintenance, or decommissioning can significantly impact emissions. As a result, addressing SF₆ emissions through better leak detection, maintenance practices, and alternative technologies, has become an important focus in reducing the climate impact of energy distribution networks.<br />"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/284_649.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>GHG Emissions from Energy Production</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Energy consumption spans four primary sectors: industry, buildings, transport, and others (including agriculture and non-energy uses). In this breakdown, emissions from electricity and heat generation are allocated to the sectors that ultimately consume them, rather than being reported separately. This approach captures both direct emissions (from burning fuels on-site) and indirect emissions (from purchased electricity and heat), providing a clearer picture of each sector’s total impact. <br /><br />After allocating electricity and heat emissions to their final use, the industry remained the largest emitting sector in 2022, contributing nearly 38% of global fuel combustion emissions. Buildings followed at 26%, with transport accounting for 24%, and the remaining emissions coming from other sectors.<br /><br />Specifically, buildings are a significant source of emissions due to their construction and increasing operational energy demands for heating, cooling, lighting, and equipment. In 2022, energy-related emissions from building operations reached 10 gigatonnes of CO2 equivalent, a 5% increase from 2020 levels and 2% higher than the pre-pandemic peak in 2019. In 2022, direct CO2 emissions from buildings such as those from on-site fossil fuel combustion for space and water heating or cooking decreased slightly. However, indirect emissions, primarily from electricity use for lighting, cooling systems, and appliances, increased. This highlights the growing reliance on electricity in buildings."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "Regional trends varied, with emissions declining in some areas and increasing in others, often influenced by factors like weather conditions. Achieving the IEA’s Net Zero Emissions (NZE) Scenario requires emissions from buildings to fall by an unprecedented 9% annually until 2030, which is a colossal challenge. High-income countries face the task of retrofitting existing buildings, while lower-income countries are grappling with the emissions implications of rapid new construction. These distinct challenges also present unique opportunities for innovation in <ai prompt=\"Clarify energy efficiency.\">energy efficiency</ai> and sustainable building practices.<br />"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "lesson_part_cover",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/251_259.webp",
          "caption": ""
        },
        "intro": "M6: The Climate Change-Energy Nexus | Chapter 1 | Lesson 1 | Part 2",
        "title": "Part 2: Impacts of Renewable Energy Development on Ecosystems"
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "This section builds on Module 3 by exploring the specific impacts of renewable energy technologies on ecosystems and strategies to mitigate them. While renewables are essential for reducing emissions, their development can disrupt habitats and biodiversity if not carefully managed. Here, we examine these challenges in greater detail and explore solutions such as sustainable siting, ecological safeguards, and integrated approaches to ensure a truly sustainable energy transition."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/284_665.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Land Use Conflicts</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "The land requirements for different energy generation methods vary significantly, especially when considering their entire life cycle. Life-cycle land use assessments account not only for the physical footprint of the energy plant itself but also for the land required for activities like mining materials for construction, sourcing fuel inputs, decommissioning, and managing waste. The construction of renewable energy facilities, such as solar farms and wind turbines, requires significantly more land to produce the same amount of electricity as a coal-fired power plant. This can lead to habitat fragmentation and displacement of wildlife.<br /><br />Ground mounted solar installations tend to have relatively high land use, although this varies based on location and the configuration of the solar panels. For instance, utility-scale solar farms, unlike rooftop solar panels, can disrupt local ecosystems by occupying large tracts of land, altering habitats, and driving species away from their natural environments. While it is true that rooftop solar installations use significantly less land, as they utilize existing structures, their land use is not zero, as mining materials for solar panel production still require land resources."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "A study analyzing solar energy land use in regions like the European Union, India, and South Korea highlight a key concern: as solar energy becomes a larger share of a region's electricity mix, associated land-use changes and emissions also grow. Solar expansion often takes place on land currently used for agriculture or forestry. This displacement can push agricultural or forestry activities to other areas, often involving unused arable land, resulting in natural land cover losses. These changes disturb local ecosystems and release carbon stored in soils and vegetation, offsetting some of the environmental benefits of solar energy.<br /><br />Another study on a 96 MW solar installation in South Africa found a significant decline in bird species diversity and density. The replacement of the natural savanna with solar panels and managed grasslands disrupted habitats, reducing nesting and foraging opportunities for local bird populations. These land-use challenges also intersect with social considerations, particularly in the context of achieving a <ai prompt=\"Clarify just energy transition.\">just energy transition</ai> (previously discussed in Module 3). Large-scale projects can have varied impacts on local communities, raising concerns about equitable access to opportunities and minimizing risks for those most affected by renewable energy expansion. Understanding the broader implications of land use in renewable energy development is crucial for designing sustainable energy strategies that minimize ecological and social impacts."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/251_451.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Infrastructure Development</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "The infrastructure required for renewable energy projects can also impact local ecosystems and livelihoods. Wind farms can pose threats to birds and bats due to collisions with turbine blades, while hydroelectric projects can disrupt aquatic habitats and alter water flow patterns. Nevertheless, research and conservation efforts are underway to identify best practices for developing energy infrastructure in a way that minimizes damage to biodiversity and ecosystem services."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/251_462.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Mining for Renewable Resources</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "The transition to renewable energy necessitates increased mining activities for essential materials like lithium, cobalt, and rare earth elements. Mining operations often result in severe environmental degradation, including soil erosion, water pollution, and destruction of habitats. In fact, many reserves of <ai prompt=\"What is critical minerals?\">critical minerals</ai> for renewable power infrastructure coincide with areas of high biodiversity value. In regions where these minerals are extracted, such as the Atacama Desert in Chile, mining activities have been attributed to being one of the major stressors to local environmental degradation. Similarly, in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), home to one of the world’s largest rainforests, cobalt mining has raised major environmental and human rights concerns, including deforestation, water contamination, and child labor. Deep sea mining is also being explored to meet mineral demand, targeting resources on the ocean floor. However, it poses serious risks to marine ecosystems, including habitat destruction and loss of biodiversity, raising concerns about its environmental impact."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "photo_full_height",
      "color_scheme": "dark",
      "content": {
        "image": {
          "src": "https://sehseadata.blob.core.windows.net/images/Modules/Module_6/257_471.webp",
          "caption": ""
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "template_id": "text",
      "color_scheme": "light",
      "content": {
        "text_elements": [
          {
            "template_id": "subtitle",
            "content": {
              "text": "<strong>Managing the Full Lifecycle of Renewable Energy Infrastructure</strong>"
            }
          },
          {
            "template_id": "paragraph_medium",
            "content": {
              "text": "The decommissioning of renewable energy infrastructure is an often-overlooked challenge that has long-term implications for energy systems and sustainability. Wind turbines, solar panels, and hydropower facilities all have finite lifespans; typically 20-30 years for solar panels and wind turbines, and up to 100 years for hydropower plants. For offshore wind farms, decommissioning involves dismantling turbines, removing subsea structures, and managing waste materials. Without proper planning, this process could disrupt marine ecosystems and create large quantities of waste. Similarly, solar panel disposal raises concerns about e-waste and the recycling of rare materials like silicon, silver, and cadmium. Hydropower plants, especially older ones, may require retrofitting or removal, which can significantly impact water systems and local ecosystems. A lack of decommissioning strategies could lead to stranded assets and increased environmental impacts."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    },
